# Project principles

Inspect existing code before adding a new implementation. Extend a current abstraction when it reasonably already covers the need.

Do not duplicate business logic, utilities, components, hooks, services, configs, types, interfaces, constants, or styles.

Avoid hardcoded domain or configuration values when the value represents configurable application or gameplay behavior. Do not promote implementation details or trivial literals into configuration.

Follow SOLID pragmatically. Prefer high cohesion and low coupling. Make dependencies explicit. Keep public contracts small. Do not cross architectural boundaries for convenience.

No global mutable state. No god classes, services, stores, or components. No speculative abstractions. No generic factories, managers, providers, wrappers, or base classes unless they remove a real duplication or coupling problem now.

Avoid static utility classes when a function, module, injected dependency, or domain object is clearer.

Before adding a dependency, confirm the project or platform does not already provide the capability.

Never perform unrelated refactors during a scoped task. Never silently change established architecture.
